WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:04.760
Picture a big storage warehouse.

2
00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:08.876
Out on the shared floor, rows of shelves hold thousands of boxes.

3
00:00:08.876 --> 00:00:12.865
And every box carries a label that says what kind of box it is.

4
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:15.597
A running Java program is that warehouse.

5
00:00:15.597 --> 00:00:19.904
The floor is the heap, where every object you create with new lives.

6
00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:23.795
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

7
00:00:23.795 --> 00:00:27.375
A Long holds one number, and that number takes 8 bytes.

8
00:00:27.375 --> 00:00:30.174
So one Long takes 8 bytes of memory, right?

9
00:00:30.174 --> 00:00:31.216
Pause and guess.

10
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:38.378
Let's find out with a box that holds nothing at all.

11
00:00:38.378 --> 00:00:40.002
new Object has no fields.

12
00:00:40.002 --> 00:00:42.340
And it still takes 16 bytes of heap.

13
00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:44.029
Those bytes are the label.

14
00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:47.255
Every object starts with a header of two parts.

15
00:00:47.255 --> 00:00:49.205
First, the mark word, 8 bytes.

16
00:00:49.205 --> 00:00:51.025
It's the housekeeping strip.

17
00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:54.266
It holds the hash code, once someone asks for it.

18
00:00:54.266 --> 00:01:00.772
The GC age: how many times the object has survived the garbage collector, the warehouse's cleaning crew.

19
00:01:00.772 --> 00:01:03.337
And the lock bits that synchronized uses.

20
00:01:03.533 --> 00:01:05.903
Second, the class pointer, 4 bytes.

21
00:01:05.903 --> 00:01:12.606
It says what kind of box this is, by pointing to the class's plan, in a back room called metaspace.

22
00:01:12.606 --> 00:01:15.991
8 plus 4: that's 12 bytes, before the first field.

23
00:01:16.133 --> 00:01:17.530
Then one more rule.

24
00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:24.437
Every object starts at a multiple of 8 bytes, so its size rounds up to the next multiple of 8.

25
00:01:24.437 --> 00:01:28.185
new Object is 12 bytes of label, plus 4 of padding.

26
00:01:28.185 --> 00:01:28.405
16.

27
00:01:29.333 --> 00:01:30.230
Let's map it.

28
00:01:30.230 --> 00:01:31.747
The box is the object.

29
00:01:31.747 --> 00:01:33.401
The label is the header.

30
00:01:33.401 --> 00:01:36.159
The housekeeping strip is the mark word.

31
00:01:36.159 --> 00:01:38.711
The kind of box is the class pointer.

32
00:01:38.711 --> 00:01:43.261
And the shelf slots, 8 bytes wide, are why everything rounds to 8.

33
00:01:44.133 --> 00:01:45.534
Now the warm-up.

34
00:01:45.534 --> 00:01:50.610
An Integer puts its 4-byte int where the padding would go.

35
00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:51.398
16 bytes.

36
00:01:51.398 --> 00:01:54.549
A Long needs 8 bytes for its number.

37
00:01:54.549 --> 00:01:57.963
12 plus 8 is 20, which rounds up to 24.

38
00:01:57.963 --> 00:01:58.488
Not 8.

39
00:01:58.488 --> 00:01:58.751
24.

40
00:01:59.667 --> 00:02:04.165
Here the warehouse picture breaks, and it's worth saying out loud.

41
00:02:04.165 --> 00:02:06.551
A paper label costs no shelf space.

42
00:02:06.551 --> 00:02:13.980
A Java header sits inside the box, and takes 12 of its bytes, whether the box holds 4 bytes of data or 4,000.

43
00:02:14.133 --> 00:02:19.056
So a Long is a sweet in a shop where the price tag costs more than the sweet.

44
00:02:19.056 --> 00:02:22.125
For small objects, the label is most of the box.

45
00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:33.397
Now a real box, from the lesson's running example: a thumbnail service that makes small previews of uploaded photos, and keeps recent ones in memory.

46
00:02:33.397 --> 00:02:35.245
A Thumbnail has six fields.

47
00:02:35.267 --> 00:02:40.466
A boolean, a long, a byte, a String, an int, and a byte array for its pixels.

48
00:02:40.466 --> 00:02:45.328
Kotlin compiles each var to the same field, so both get the same layout.

49
00:02:45.467 --> 00:02:49.115
So will the fields sit in memory in the order we wrote them?

50
00:02:49.115 --> 00:02:50.088
Pause and guess.

51
00:02:53.867 --> 00:02:59.382
Here's what JOL, a small OpenJDK tool, prints on JDK 25.

52
00:02:59.382 --> 00:03:02.435
The header fills bytes 0 to 11.

53
00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:06.240
Then the JVM did something the source never asked for.

54
00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:08.869
It moved width, the int, up to byte 12.

55
00:03:08.869 --> 00:03:12.980
A long must start at a multiple of 8, and wouldn't fit there.

56
00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:14.531
So the long goes at 16.

57
00:03:14.667 --> 00:03:19.887
Then the two 1-byte fields, a 2-byte gap, and the two references, 4 bytes each.

58
00:03:19.887 --> 00:03:22.531
Four bytes of padding end the box at 40.

59
00:03:22.667 --> 00:03:28.330
The JVM is a tidy packer, the kind who repacks your suitcase while you're not looking.

60
00:03:28.330 --> 00:03:30.701
Your code can't tell the difference.

61
00:03:30.701 --> 00:03:32.479
6 of the 40 bytes are gaps.

62
00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:34.565
So here's the rule.

63
00:03:34.565 --> 00:03:38.877
An object costs its header plus its fields, rounded up to 8.

64
00:03:38.877 --> 00:03:40.601
Count objects, not data.

65
00:03:41.733 --> 00:03:45.305
Counting objects is where the surprises live.

66
00:03:45.305 --> 00:03:55.464
The file extension jpg, as a String, costs 48 bytes: a 24-byte String object, plus a 24-byte array that holds the three letters.

67
00:03:55.600 --> 00:04:09.289
And the numbers 0 to 999 in an ArrayList of Integer cost 20,976 bytes, because every number is its own 16-byte box, plus a 4-byte reference in the list.

68
00:04:09.289 --> 00:04:13.341
An int array holds the same numbers in 4,016.

69
00:04:14.867 --> 00:04:15.983
Did you notice?

70
00:04:15.983 --> 00:04:20.747
Every reference took 4 bytes, but a full 64-bit address takes 8.

71
00:04:21.533 --> 00:04:23.788
The trick follows from the padding.

72
00:04:23.788 --> 00:04:29.649
Every box starts at a multiple of 8, so the lowest 3 bits of every address are always zero.

73
00:04:29.649 --> 00:04:31.388
The JVM doesn't store them.

74
00:04:31.533 --> 00:04:36.805
It stores a short shelf number instead, and multiplies it by 8 when it follows it.

75
00:04:36.805 --> 00:04:38.733
That's called compressed oops.

76
00:04:38.733 --> 00:04:40.919
Oop means ordinary object pointer.

77
00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:47.871
A 32-bit number counts about 4 billion slots of 8 bytes: that's 32 GB.

78
00:04:47.871 --> 00:04:52.121
So under a 32 GB heap, a reference takes 4 bytes.

79
00:04:52.121 --> 00:04:56.284
Give the JVM 40 GB, and references go back to 8.

80
00:04:56.467 --> 00:05:01.238
The Thumbnail grows to 48 bytes, and the list grows by 24 percent.

81
00:05:01.238 --> 00:05:03.840
A bigger heap, full of bigger boxes.

82
00:05:04.733 --> 00:05:06.909
So, does a Long take 8 bytes?

83
00:05:06.909 --> 00:05:07.134
No.

84
00:05:07.134 --> 00:05:12.535
It takes 24: a 12-byte label, the 8-byte number, and 4 bytes of padding.

85
00:05:12.535 --> 00:05:17.261
When memory matters, count the boxes, not the data inside them.

86
00:05:18.133 --> 00:05:19.019
Quick check.

87
00:05:19.019 --> 00:05:22.711
A subclass of Thumbnail adds a String and a short.

88
00:05:22.711 --> 00:05:24.040
How big is it now?

89
00:05:24.040 --> 00:05:25.221
Pause and guess.

90
00:05:28.467 --> 00:05:29.076
Still 40.

91
00:05:29.076 --> 00:05:35.307
The String's 4 bytes and the short's 2 slide into the gaps the padding had already paid for.
